== Verification

The code is docked against five models with known behaviour:

+ plain neighbour averaging (DeGroot) --- consensus on any connected non-bipartite graph
+ Friedkin--Johnsen --- the analytic equilibrium
+ Hegselmann--Krause --- the known $epsilon$-dependence of the cluster count
+ mean-field fixed point of the alarm recursion
+ empty graph --- full and null runs coincide, $Pi equiv 0$

*14 / 14 checks pass.* The Saltelli/Jansen estimator is written directly and docked
against the Ishigami function to within 0.0025.

Randomness: one recorded seed, three named substreams (setup, targeting, noise).
Sharing a stream between targeting and noise would desynchronise the four arms and
destroy the counterfactual.

== The anchor, and why $sigma = 0$

#align(center)[
  #table(
    columns: 4,
    inset: 6pt,
    align: center,
    stroke: none,
    table.hline(),
    table.header([$sigma$], [predicted $-1 slash ln(1 - sigma)$], [measured persistence],
                 [$Pi$ 250 steps after withdrawal]),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.4pt),
    [0.00], [$infinity$], [censored], [0.179],
    [0.05], [19.5], [13], [0.005],
    [0.10], [9.5], [9], [0.007],
    [0.20], [4.5], [4], [---],
    table.hline(),
  )
]

The anchor is a restoring force toward each agent's *pre-campaign* position, which
under a consensual start sits near zero. Any positive $sigma$ dissolves the
manufactured division on its own timescale, whatever $omega$ is: the reverse of
H2. Fixing it positive and leaving it outside the analysis would have handed the
whole result to a constant nobody was looking at.
